  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 152 AM MDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nChances for precipitation will be widespread across the CWA today. \nThe threat for heavy rain and localized flash flooding is still in \nthe forecast, with areas east of the Divide having the better \nchances. Precipitable water values will be higher here, ranging \nbetween 1 and 1.3\". WPC Excessive Rainfall Outlook continues to have \na Slight risk in the forecast for Johnson County and portions of \nNatrona County (mainly along and east of I-25). Showers will move \nnorthward over Lincoln County and the Green River Basin after \nsunrise and spread over western portions through the rest of the \nmorning. Scattered showers and thunderstorms will develop across the \nrest of the CWA between 18Z and 20Z, moving toward the northeast \nthrough the afternoon. Convection will begin to decrease by 00Z, \nending from southwest to northeast through the evening. Light \nshowers will still be over northern portions by 06Z and linger \nthrough the rest of the night. Thunderstorms will be possible across \nthe area this afternoon, with portions of Lincoln and Sublette \ncounties having the best chances as a shortwave from NV/UT \nmoves over this region. \n\nConvection will not be as extensive Friday, being more scattered \nthrough the day along and north of a Afton to Midwest line. Heavy \nrain and localized flash flooding will continue to be the main \nthreat from these storms as PWAT values remain abnormally high, \nranging between 0.7\" to 1.1\". The hazy/smoky skies looks to no \nlonger be an issue by Friday, as the southwest flow aloft cuts off \nthe source region and the atmosphere becomes more mixed. \n\nThe cooler and wetter pattern will continue through the \nweekend, with showers and thunderstorms possible each day. The \nthreat for heavy rain will not be as strong by Sunday, as a bit \nmore drier air filters into the region. More drier conditions \nlook to return by Monday, but temperatures are trending to \nremain seasonal for the early part of next week.",
